/*!
    \page creator-developing-harmonyos.html
    \previouspage creator-reference.html

    \title Developing for HarmonyOS devices

    \quotation
    \l {HarmonyOS}{How to: Develop for HarmonyOS}
    \endquotation

    \summary {Build, deploy, run, and debug applications on HarmonyOS devices.}

    \QC builds applications for HarmonyOS devices, packages them as a HarmonyOS
    Ability Package (\c {.hap}), signs and installs the package, and runs and
    debugs the application on the device.

    Set the location of the HarmonyOS SDK, and \QC derives the rest from it: the
    compilers appear as toolchains, connected devices are detected over \c hdc,
    and a \l {Qt for HarmonyOS} produces a kit of its own.

    \section1 What you need

    \list
        \li The HarmonyOS command-line tools, version 6.1 or newer, or an
            installation of DevEco Studio. The directory to point \QC at is the
            one that has both \c sdk and \c bin in it. The public OpenHarmony
            SDK is not a substitute: it carries no \c hvigor, which packaging
            an application needs.
        \li A Qt for HarmonyOS. Qt Online Installer and Qt Maintenance Tool
            offer one as \c {qt.qt6.<version>.harmonyos_arm64_v8a}, from Qt
            6.12 on. \QC recognizes it by its \c ohos-clang mkspec. For a Qt
            from a development branch, or for modules the package leaves out,
            the \c {scripts/harmonyos/build_qt.py} script in the \QC sources
            cross-builds one.
        \li A Java runtime. The SDK signs the package with
            \c hap-sign-tool.jar, which needs one.
        \li The third-party libraries Qt links against that the device does not
            carry, such as ICU, \c fontconfig, \c freetype, and \c libpng,
            built for HarmonyOS and installed under one prefix. The installed
            package carries none of them, so it needs them as much as a Qt
            built here does. The \c {scripts/harmonyos/install_third_party.py}
            script in the \QC sources builds them with vcpkg.
        \li Signing material issued for a developer account: a certificate, a
            provisioning profile, a keystore, and the key alias. A provisioning
            profile is issued for one bundle name and, for debugging, for
            particular devices. The device installs a package only under the
            bundle name of the profile it was signed with.
        \li A device with wireless debugging turned on, paired with the
            computer.
    \endlist

    \sa {HarmonyOS}{How to: Develop for HarmonyOS},
    {Manage kits}{How to: Manage kits}
*/

/*!
    \page creator-how-to-set-up-harmonyos.html
    \previouspage creator-how-tos.html

    \ingroup creator-how-to-harmonyos
    \ingroup creator-how-to-sdks

    \title Set up the HarmonyOS SDK

    \list 1
        \li Go to \preferences > \uicontrol SDKs > \uicontrol HarmonyOS.
        \li In \uicontrol {HarmonyOS SDK location}, enter the directory that
            has the \c sdk and \c bin directories in it, or select
            \uicontrol {Auto-detect} to look for one in the usual places. The
            label below the field says what was found.
        \li To install the public OpenHarmony SDK into that directory instead,
            select \uicontrol Download. It covers the compilers, the sysroot,
            and \c hdc, which is what building for a device and reaching one
            take. Packaging an application still needs \c hvigor from Huawei's
            command-line tools. The archive it comes in is a few gigabytes.
        \li In \uicontrol {Additional packages}, enter the installation prefix
            of the third-party libraries built for HarmonyOS, which is the
            directory their \c lib is in. \QC passes it to the build as
            \c QT_ADDITIONAL_PACKAGES_PREFIX_PATH, which adds the libraries
            into the package. Without them, the application installs and
            starts but cannot load the platform plugin.
        \li In \uicontrol {Device-hosted SDK}, enter the root of an
            OpenHarmony SDK whose own tools run on a device. Huawei publishes
            it as \c native-ohos-x64, and its payload is for arm64 despite
            that name. \QC takes a compiler, a CMake, and a sysroot out of it
            and adds them into its own HarmonyOS package, which is what lets
            a \QC that runs on a device build there. Leave the field empty
            for a \QC that only builds on the host.
        \li Keep \uicontrol {Create kits automatically} selected to have \QC
            offer a kit for every Qt for HarmonyOS it knows about.
        \li Building for HarmonyOS needs a Qt that was cross-built for it. The
            Qt installer publishes one for Qt 6.12 or later, and
            \uicontrol {Install Qt for HarmonyOS} installs it. A Qt for
            HarmonyOS that is there already but unknown to \QC is registered
            instead.
        \li Where there is no \QOI, which is the case for a \QC that
            runs on a device, the same button downloads the Qt from the
            repository that \uicontrol {Qt repository} names. That is the
            directory an \c Updates.xml describing the Qt packages is
            published in, and the newest Qt for HarmonyOS it offers is installed.
    \endlist

    Once the SDK location is set, the compilers in it appear in
    \preferences > \uicontrol Kits > \uicontrol Compilers, and a device that
    \c hdc reports appears in \preferences > \uicontrol Devices >
    \uicontrol Devices, without any further configuration.

    That toolchain is around 500 MB of the package it is included in. Configure
    \QC with \c {-DQTC_HARMONYOS_DEVICE_TOOLCHAIN=OFF} to leave it out.

    The package does not include the Qt tools, because Qt cannot build them for
    HarmonyOS yet. A \QC running on a device therefore needs a Qt that was
    built on that device, registered once in \preferences >
    \uicontrol Kits > \uicontrol {Qt Versions}. \QC completes the kit for
    the device from it.

    The \c {scripts/harmonyos/install_sdk.py} script in the \QC sources
    installs the same SDK from the command line, and takes the release and the
    components to unpack as arguments.

    \sa {Developing for HarmonyOS devices}, {HarmonyOS}{How to: Develop for
    HarmonyOS}, {Sign HarmonyOS packages}
*/

/*!
    \page creator-how-to-sign-harmonyos-packages.html
    \previouspage creator-how-tos.html

    \ingroup creator-how-to-harmonyos
    \ingroup creator-how-to-sdks

    \title Sign HarmonyOS packages

    A HarmonyOS device installs only a signed package, so the signing material
    is not optional. It is issued for a developer account and cannot be
    generated locally.

    \list 1
        \li Go to \preferences > \uicontrol SDKs > \uicontrol HarmonyOS >
            \uicontrol {Package Signing}.
        \li Set \uicontrol {Certificate (.cer)},
            \uicontrol {Provisioning profile (.p7b)},
            \uicontrol {Keystore (.p12)}, and \uicontrol {Key alias} to what
            the account issued.
        \li Enter \uicontrol {Key password} and
            \uicontrol {Keystore password}. \QC keeps them in the system
            keychain. They can also be passed in the environment as
            \c QT_HARMONYOS_SIGNING_KEY_PASSWORD and
            \c QT_HARMONYOS_SIGNING_STORE_PASSWORD, which is what a build
            machine would do. Only the passwords fall back to the environment,
            so the other four have to be set here.
    \endlist

    \QC reads the provisioning profile before it packages, and reports what it
    can see there as issues:

    \list
        \li It packages under the bundle name the profile is for, and says so
            when that is not the name the project asked for.
        \li It drops the restricted permissions the profile does not list under
            \c allowed-acls, which the device would otherwise refuse the whole
            package for, and names them, so they can be added to the profile.
        \li It reports a profile whose validity expired or has not started
            yet.
        \li It reports a device whose UDID the profile does not list, which is
            what a debug profile installs by.
    \endlist

    \sa {Developing for HarmonyOS devices}, {HarmonyOS}{How to: Develop for
    HarmonyOS}, {Set up the HarmonyOS SDK}
*/

/*!
    \page creator-how-to-add-harmonyos-devices.html
    \previouspage creator-how-tos.html

    \ingroup creator-how-to-harmonyos
    \ingroup creator-how-to-manage-kits

    \title Connect HarmonyOS devices

    \QC finds HarmonyOS devices through the \c hdc of the SDK, so a device that
    \c hdc lists needs no configuration of its own.

    \list 1
        \li Turn on wireless debugging on the device, and note the address and
            port it reports. The port changes every time.
        \li Connect to it once from the command line, with the \c hdc of the
            SDK:
            \badcode
            hdc tconn <address>:<port>
            hdc list targets
            \endcode
        \li Go to \preferences > \uicontrol Devices > \uicontrol Devices. The
            device is there, and its page reports it as ready to use.
    \endlist

    A HarmonyOS device is also offered as a build device, for a project whose
    sources and toolchain are on the device itself.

    \section1 Create a kit

    Register the Qt for HarmonyOS in \preferences > \uicontrol Kits >
    \uicontrol {Qt Versions} > \uicontrol Add, and select its \c qmake. \QC
    creates a kit for it, with the device as the run device and the compilers
    from the SDK, and no further editing is needed.

    \sa {Developing for HarmonyOS devices}, {HarmonyOS}{How to: Develop for
    HarmonyOS}, {Manage kits}{How to: Manage kits}, {Managing kits}
*/
